<section id="s-variance" data-code="variance">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Variance Trap</div>
<h2>The Late-Stage Signal Collapse in Output-Space OPD</h2>
<p>On-policy distillation (OPD) aligns a student language model with a teacher model on trajectories sampled from the student policy. This mitigates the **exposure bias** that plagues supervised fine-tuning. However, because vocabulary sizes |V| are extremely large (e.g. |V| ≈ 150K for the Qwen series), computing the full-vocabulary KL divergence at every token position is computationally prohibitive.</p>

<p>To make training feasible, production systems approximate the loss using the **sampled-token estimator**, computing next-token probability log-ratios only on the student-generated token y<sub>t</sub> ∼ p<sub>t</sub>:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Sampled-Token OPD Objective & Gradient</div>
  L<sub>OPD</sub>(θ) = E<sub>ŷ∼π<sub>θ</sub></sub> [ log p<sub>t</sub>(ŷ<sub>t</sub>) - log q<sub>t</sub>(ŷ<sub>t</sub>) ] <br>
  ∇<sub>θ</sub> L<sub>OPD</sub>(θ) = E<sub>ŷ∼π<sub>θ</sub></sub> [ (log p<sub>t</sub>(ŷ<sub>t</sub>) - log q<sub>t</sub>(ŷ<sub>t</sub>)) ∇<sub>θ</sub> log p<sub>t</sub>(ŷ<sub>t</sub>) ]
</div>

<p>This formulation has a fatal mathematical flaw: it is a **single-sample Monte Carlo estimator** of the policy gradient (similar to REINFORCE). As the student model policy converges toward the teacher (p<sub>t</sub> → q<sub>t</sub>), the expected gradient signal shrinks to zero. However, the variance of the sampled-token estimator is bounded below by the Fisher information times the log-ratio variance. This variance **does not decay** as training progresses. Consequently, in the late stages of training, the optimization signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) collapses to zero, driving the policy off-course and causing the performance of the student to plateau below the teacher.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">gradient-variance-simulator.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to run training steps and observe SNR and accuracy curves</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="chart-controls">
      <button class="chart-btn active" id="sim-opd1-btn">OPD Top-1 (Sampled)</button>
      <button class="chart-btn" id="sim-op16-btn">OPD Top-16</button>
      <button class="chart-btn" id="sim-oprd-btn">OPRD (Ours)</button>
      <button class="chart-btn" id="run-sim-btn" style="border-color: var(--cyan); color: var(--cyan); margin-left: auto;">Play Simulation</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="variance-canvas" style="width: 100%; height: 260px; background: #0b0b0e; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px;"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>As simulated above, under **Sampled-token OPD**, the gradient variance remains high while the true gradient signal shrinks, causing accuracy to plateau early. **OPRD** provides a deterministic MSE objective at the representation level: its gradient contains zero conditional sampling variance, enabling monotonic accuracy gains that steadily close the gap to the teacher.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-bottleneck" data-code="bottleneck">
<div class="section-no">02 — The LM Head Bottleneck</div>
<h2>The Spectral Null Space of Output-Space Losses</h2>
<p>Informational limits also restrict output-space distillation. In any output-space variant, the teacher is treated as a black-box probability oracle. The loss functions match probabilities p<sub>t</sub> and q<sub>t</sub> produced by the language model head W<sub>head</sub> ∈ ℝ<sup>|V| × d</sup> acting on the final hidden state h<sub>t</sub> ∈ ℝ<sup>d</sup>. This ignores the entire stack of intermediate hidden representations that the teacher computed.</p>

<p>Because the vocabulary dimension |V| is much larger than the hidden dimension d, the projection mapping is highly low-rank and ill-conditioned. Additionally, the softmax function is invariant to additive constant shifts. We can formalize this loss-blind region as the **effective null space** of the LM head:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Effective Null Space of the LM Head</div>
  N<sub>W</sub> = {"{"} Δh ∈ ℝ<sup>d</sup> : W<sub>head</sub> Δh ∈ span{"{"}1{"}"} {"}"}
</div>

<p>For any student representation h<sub>θ</sub> and teacher representation h<sub>T</sub>, if their difference lies within this null space (h<sub>θ</sub> - h<sub>T</sub> ∈ N<sub>W</sub>), the resulting next-token probability distribution is *exactly* identical. Thus, any output-space loss is precisely **zero**, leaving large directions of the hidden-state space unconstrained. Near the boundary, deviations along low-singular-value right-singular vectors v<sub>d</sub> can be orders of magnitude larger than along top-singular directions while yielding the same loss. Output-space objectives are blind to these internal representational misalignments.</p>

