<p>As you slide the offset, the student hidden state h<sub>θ</sub> deviates significantly from the teacher h<sub>T</sub> along the null-space direction. However, the output probabilities remain identical (zero KL error). Output-space OPD cannot correct this alignment error, whereas OPRD directly penalizes the MSE discrepancy, ensuring representation-level congruence.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-oprd" data-code="oprd_loss">
<div class="section-no">03 — Hidden Representation Distillation</div>
<h2>Moving Supervision from Output to Input</h2>
<p>To resolve both the variance trap and the information bottleneck, we propose **On-Policy Representation Distillation (OPRD)**. Instead of querying logits after the LM head, OPRD extracts intermediate representations h<sub>t</sub><sup>(l)</sup> across selected layers and positions on the same on-policy rollouts, bypassing the LM head entirely.</p>

<p>Given a set of distilled layers L<sub>layer</sub> ⊆ {"{"}1, ..., L{"}"} and a position mask m<sub>t</sub> ∈ {"{"}0, 1{"}"} identifying response positions P(ŷ) to supervise, OPRD minimizes the masked mean-squared error:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">OPRD Loss Function</div>
  L<sub>OPRD</sub>(θ) = E<sub>x, ŷ</sub> [ (1 / (∑ m<sub>t</sub>)) ∑<sub>t=1</sub><sup>T</sup> m<sub>t</sub> · (1 / |L<sub>layer</sub>|) ∑<sub>l∈L<sub>layer</sub></sub> (1/d) || h<sub>θ, t</sub><sup>(l)</sup> - sg(h<sub>T, t</sub><sup>(l)</sup>) ||<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> ]
</div>

<p>Here, sg(·) represents the stop-gradient operator applied to the teacher representations. When the student and teacher models have different hidden widths (d<sub>s</sub> ≠ d<sub>T</sub>), we introduce a lightweight linear projector W ∈ ℝ<sup>d<sub>T</sub> × d<sub>s</sub></sup> on the student representations, which is trained jointly with the backbone. This objective provides dense, deterministic supervision: its gradient is a direct function of the intermediate activations, carrying zero additional sampling variance and exposing orders of magnitude more scalar signals than output probabilities.</p>

