<p>By default, OPRD aligns representations uniformly across the network. If we adjust the slider to distill specific layers, we notice that early layers are already highly aligned (>99%), whereas late layers show the largest divergence. This indicates that representation-level alignment is most critical in the late blocks of the model.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-masking" data-code="suffix_mask">
<div class="section-no">04 — Target the Tail</div>
<h2>Divergence Concentrates in the Suffix</h2>
<p>A natural design question is: *which response positions should be supervised by OPRD?* For reasoning models generating long chain-of-thought (CoT) responses, supervising every single token is memory-intensive. To optimize compute, we analyze the spatial distribution of student-teacher representational divergence on student rollouts.</p>

<p>Measuring the cosine similarity between student and teacher representations reveals a striking pattern: the student's hidden states are highly aligned with the teacher's at the beginning of the response (>97% similarity for the first 1600 tokens). This is because the early prompt-following preamble is straightforward. However, the similarity drops significantly (<92%) at the tail of the response, precisely where the model commits to a final answer.</p>

